# Prefer DSP Packages

Before implementing a capability in any DSP project, check whether an approved DSP package already provides it. Prefer reusing or extending the shared package over creating project-specific duplicate functionality.

Consider these packages:

- [file-management](https://github.com/Deepsink-Partners/file-management)
- [laravel-emails](https://github.com/Deepsink-Partners/laravel-emails)
- [laravel-push-notifications](https://github.com/Deepsink-Partners/laravel-push-notifications)
- [laravel-secure](https://github.com/Deepsink-Partners/laravel-secure)
- [laravel-social-auth](https://github.com/Deepsink-Partners/laravel-social-auth)
- [payfast](https://github.com/Deepsink-Partners/payfast)
- [laravel-payments](https://github.com/Deepsink-Partners/laravel-payments)

## Required Process

1. Inspect the relevant package documentation and current API before designing a replacement; use a local checkout under `packages/` when available.
2. Confirm that the package supports the project's framework version, runtime, security requirements, and required behavior.
3. Integrate through the package's documented public API; do not copy package internals into an application.
4. If the package nearly fits, prefer a backward-compatible shared extension over an application-only reimplementation. Obtain explicit permission before changing another repository.
5. After fixing an issue in a DSP package, identify other DSP projects that consume the affected package or version and determine whether they need the same application-level fix, dependency update, configuration change, or migration.
6. Present the affected projects and proposed changes, obtain explicit permission, then apply and verify the corresponding fix in each approved project.
7. If no package is suitable, document why before implementing project-specific functionality.

Do not force a package when it is incompatible, insecure, unmaintained, or cannot meet the requirements safely.

## AI Docs Tracking

- Track every DSP package investigation, integration, fix, compatibility decision, affected consumer, validation result, release requirement, and next step in the relevant file under `ai-docs/`.
- Update an existing package or project document when possible; otherwise create a clearly named document. Never record secrets or credentials.
- Commit every `ai-docs` update in the `ai-docs` repository and push it to `origin/main` before completing the Agent interaction.
- If the commit or push fails, report the failure clearly and do not claim that the documentation is synchronized.
